// onsa.toml
// [test]
// spec = ["§6.4", "§6.5", "§4.6", "§18.1"]
// fixes = 1

// What the candidate of a derive written as an attribute does with names that Onsa does not derive (spec §6.4, §4.6,
// S-34, S-463). The names are copied as they are: `Debug` and `Clone` are the names of other languages, and the
// candidate does not translate them. The candidate leaves out only the kinds `Copy` and `Dup`, which cannot be
// written in the head of a declaration (§4.6), and the rule for that is the one of Onsa, whichever language the
// attribute comes from. A name that is not in the closed list of §6.4 is E0416 in the head of the declaration, so
// after the candidate the declaration has that error of a later stage, and no error of the syntax stage
// (§18.1: a candidate leaves no error of the same or an earlier stage). The note of that E0416 shows that the
// implementation is written by hand (spec §6.4). The main position is the attribute. One error in each
// declaration unit (P-01).

@derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq) pub struct Everything { //~ E0020
  id: U32,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)] pub struct RustStyle { //~ E0020
  id: U32,
}

#[derive(Debug)] pub struct DebugOnly {             //~ E0020
  id: U32,
}

@derive(Clone, Copy) pub enum CloneAndCopy {        //~ E0020
  Off,
  On,
}

#[derive(Debug, Eq)] pub struct Already: PartialEq { //~ E0020
  id: U32,
}

@derive(Clone) pub struct Generic[T] {              //~ E0020
  value: T,
}
